Date: Tue, 26 Oct 1993 07:56:11 -0400
From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C McCluskey)
Subject: Neural Networks (was Beating the Stock Market?)

 derek@cs.wisc.edu  (Derek Zahn) writes (in X-Message-Number:#93-10-807):
>some of the tweaks are quite helpful.  As to cascade correlation,
>I've never been very impressed with its ability to solve problems
>with very large numbers of "input units" because of the epicycle-
>style way new free parameters are added to the model.
 
 As far as I can tell, all existing neural net algorithms are prone
epicycle-style learning, but some do it better than others. To get
the kind of paridigm shift needed to find the Copernican solution
we need something more than gradient descent, probably more like
genetic programming (although current attempts at GP operate on
components that are too low level to produce that kind of paradigm
shift).
 To phrase the difference between cascade correlation and standard
backprop in epicycle-style terminology, cascade correlation typically
trains the first hidden unit to reproduce the most obvious cycle, then
trains the next hidden unit to add the next most obvious cycle, etc.
Whereas algorithms that train all hidden units in parallel start by
training a lot of the hidden units to reproduce the most obvious cycle,
then as the entire set of hidden units succeeds at predicting the
most obvious cycle, a number of less successfull hidden units get
retrained to add in the next most obvious cycle, etc., resulting in
concepts being spread out over a much larger number of units in standard
backprop than in cascade correlation.
(Although this would probably make cascade correlation less useful
for a biological implementation at the single neuron level because
it would not withstand damage as well).

