Ray Cromwell writes:
>   Could anti-biotech memes simply be a leftover survival mechanism (selfish
> genes)? It seems most people are universually opposed to designer humans.

Perhaps, but I think that much of the current distaste for genetic research
arises from the early Nazi perversion of the theory of genetics into their
"master race" drivel. Plus, you'll always hear objections to this sort of
thing from those who call it "playing god." Let's not forget that the
vast majority of humanity finds it unnatural to imagine life without being
subject to some kind of supernatural authority and are frightened by anyone
who doesn't share their particular delusion.

During the 50's and 60's, we went through a period during which our culture(s)
clearly echoed a popular fear of computers. They were thought to be on
the verge of throwing people out of work, making humans idle and useless,
and so on. This fear has largely vanished (or been seriously diminished)
because widespread access to computer technology has enabled people to
see the benefits and compare them (quasi-)rationally with the supposed dangers.

Now we're seeing a spate of anti-biotech fears (especially anti-gene tech)
in the form of movies like "Jurrassic Park", "Man's Best Friend" (check
for it in your local theatres, it looks unbelievably bad), etc. This too
will pass when advanced bio-tech and genetic therapy start saving people's
lives in a major way. I expect that we have a handful of years to go before
people start seeing the real, concrete benefits and start comparing them
with the Chicken Little pronouncements. When they do, movies about the
dangers of bio-tech will seem as quaint and outdated as movies about
egomanical computers.

