S >give, and how does one get it?  Is there a minimal age for this status?

You must have missed my talk at San Francisco's Gorilla Grotto in 1979 
"Free *Your* Slaves -- Why Parents Should Emancipate Their Children." 

There are four ways for children to be emancipated:

1) By declaration of the parents
2) By acquiescence of the parents to the child's leaving home
3) By court action
4) By reaching the age of majority.  Now 18 in most (all?) states.

1), 2), and 3) have no specific age limits although a court is unlikely to 
emancipate or recognize the emancipation of children younger than 16.

You can emancipate your 10 year old by declaration (sample form in most 
general legal form books), but you still have to support him.  
Emancipation *does* have psychological significance especially if you make 
a ceremony of it.  Libertarian parents should emancipate their children 
for consistency.  (TJ and slaves, you know).  Such emancipation does have 
legal effect in that by it you renounce all your claims on the income of 
your minor children.  At common law, parents had a right to the labor and 
income of their minor children.  These days, they usually have to hold 
such funds "in trust" for the kid but they can blow some for reasonable 
support expenses.

By all means, emancipate your kid, give him the certificate and he can 
show it to whoever he likes.  Might blow their minds.  "I'm free, you're a 
slave."  Hope he wouldn't be too rough on others.

The courts would not really attach any significance to such as act (except 
as regards income) but it might be evidence actual emancipation when he 
gets older.

Duncan Frissell

