From: John K Clark <johnkc@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Free Will

On Fri,22 Oct 1993 (Stephen J. Whitrow) sjr@liberty.demon.co.uk Wrote:
			      
		>it's not difficult to imagine that these memecomplexes are
		>not totally determined by environment (or even genetically!)
		
Yes, I can also imagine that but I don't think this de-coupling
from environmental causation has much to do with creativity and
I am absolutely sure it has nothing to do with rational thought.
( Please note I said I was "absolutely sure" I did not
say I was correct, a very different thing. A person can be
absolutely positively 100% certain of something and still be
dead wrong, in fact it's very common.) 

		> I suspect an individual's memetic development is rather
		>Lamarckian.
		
Excellent point. It's a pity nature never thought of how
acquired characteristics could be inherited. The two parts of
Darwinian Evolution, random mutation and deterministic natural
selection, work  but its horribly slow, it took 15 billion years
( give or take a few billion) to produce us. Fortunately we
found a better way in Human Thought. Memes evolve mostly by
Lamarckian not Darwinian methods , and that's why Social
Evolution is about 100 million times faster than physical
evolution and its accelerating. Randomness plays no important
constructive part although natural selection has a strong
supporting role. I don't see how randomness can be our friend
any more than entropy can be, except in a few minor cases in
statistics.
		
		> under the causality axiom, caused events are unfree and
		>random events are free, so we've either got "will" or
		>"freedom" and not the two together.
		
Even if the world were totally deterministic (and we know it
isn't) we would still have a feeling of choice and we could
still be FREE to do what we want to do ; it's just that "what we
want to do" would be caused by something else.
		
	   >We can also choose to make random arational decisions if we
	   >want, and this is "free will".
	   
Could be true but it's not very useful because it doesn't really
answer anything, it just kicks the problem upstairs. How do we
choose to make random arational decisions? It's like religious
people who say "God must exist because something must have made
the Universe" but they never think to ask the question "Who made
God?".
	   
		>purpose and order can arise spontaneously.  It doesn't have
		>to be created, as Darwin showed us.  
		
When talking about purpose you have to ask " purpose for who?".
What is the purpose of a violin? To the manufacturer the purpose
was to get a paycheck. To a drowning man it's purpose was to act
as a life-preserver. To a musician it's purpose was to make
music and to his tone deaf child it's purpose was to be used to
swat a wasp.

I agree that order can arise spontaneously but something can be
unplanned and still be deterministic. When water freezes nobody
planed it and randomness was not involved yet the atoms
organized into an ordered lattice. Because Darwinian evolution
has a random component it  is inherently inefficient and cruel.
I much prefer a deterministic process to produce order, like
Lamarckian evolution. We can do things better than nature did,
and speaking of nature ...

		>Electricity is involved in the brain, but I think the
		>difference is that having electrodes                    
		>connected up whilst undergoing brain surgery is not 'natural'
		
Neither is talking on the telephone, using a computer, watching
TV or reading a book yet these things change our thoughts,
desires and behavior. If ,like the electrodes, they just give us
a "illusion" of free will, how can we tell if were ever really
free? I don't much like natural things myself, after all nothing
is more natural than death.
		
		> the functionalists believe a science of conscious
		>intelligence can claim a discontinuous autonomy from 
		>established natural sciences such as physics and
		>biology on the grounds of a unique feature.
		
I don't think that's quite right but it may contain a particle
of truth. Your very  interesting ideas about the many minds
model of Quantum Mechanics gives me the courage to write some
wild  speculation , but I'm not sure I even agree with much of
it .An early version of Microsoft Flight Simulator had a bug ,
if you flew upside down and pushed the stick forward you would
fall up. Did the laws of Physics really change? Yes and no, it
depends on what level your talking about. To the objects in the
simulated world the laws of physics did indeed change, you can
make them anything you want. To the objects in our own world
nothing changes. Perhaps consciousness is like this. At the
level of  quantum jumps or neuron firing my mind is not real but
to another conscious being it is real because were both on the
same level. Our entire universe could be a simulation, the
strangeness in Quantum Mechanics could be the result of flaws or
lack of resolution in some vast cellular automation that we only
notice if we look too closely. 
		
In a similar way we could say that nothing means anything
without an interpretation. imagine a parallel world in which the
people use the same English words we do but they mean different
things, sky means dog, beauty means potato etc. In one such
world this post would mean a vigorous defense of socialism in
another it would mean hard core pornography and in another it
would mean the operating instructions for a new type of can
opener. The words and the shape of the letters are the same in
all cases but they really don't mean anything, its just a bunch
of squiggles, until you use an interpretation, in this case the
rules of language. Change the interpretation and the meaning
changes.
		
Hans Moravec calls these ideas "Universal Realization" and is
currently writing a book about it. His main points:
		
1) Anything can simulate anything.

2) A simulated object is a real object in it's own world.

3) Nothing means anything without an interpretation.

4) A thing can have more than one interpretation.

5) A mind is the one thing that has it's own self consistent
interpretation inherent within it; in fact that is the
definition of a mind (although you would still need an
interpretation to make contact with it).

Concerning #5, to communicate with another person the
interpretation is straightforward, if the person smiles he is
happy, if they make sounds with their mouth we find meaning in
them from the rules of grammar, but Moravec goes as far as to
say that a interpretation must exist that can find a mind even
in a rock. The reason we can't detect them is that were too
stupid. The interpretation is so hideously complex that as a
practical matter their workings are indistinguishable from
random thermal vibrations.

Minds in rocks? Well... maybe.  
			       John K Clark              johnkc@well.sf.ca.us




